\title{Base R Fourfold Display Layer Processor}
\description{
Processes Base R \code{fourfoldplot()} layers drawn with
\code{std = "ind.max"} or \code{std = "all.max"} -- a 2x2 contingency table drawn as
four quarter-circles, one per cell, sized so the wedge's AREA is
proportional to that cell's count.

Read as a \code{heat} layer, for the same reason \code{assocplot()} is: the drawing
is a 2x2 grid of one number per cell, and row-then-column is how a reader
navigates a contingency table. Measured on
\code{fourfoldplot(as.table(matrix(c(10, 40, 90, 160), 2)), std = "ind.max")},
the radii and the counts they recover:

\preformatted{
grob                       r        quadrant      r^2 * max(count)  cell
graphics-plot-1-polygon-1  0.250000 upper LEFT     10               [1, 1]
graphics-plot-1-polygon-2  0.500000 lower LEFT     40               [2, 1]
graphics-plot-1-polygon-3  0.750000 upper RIGHT    90               [1, 2]
graphics-plot-1-polygon-4  1.000000 lower RIGHT   160               [2, 2]
}

exactly, so \code{radius / sqrt(count)} is one constant (\code{0.0790569415} here, with a
relative spread of \code{1.755e-16}). The table's FIRST dimension runs
vertically and its SECOND horizontally -- the transpose of \code{assocplot()}'s
arrangement, and measured from the label grobs, which put
\code{names(dimnames(x))[1]} at the top and bottom of the drawing and
\code{names(dimnames(x))[2]} at the left and right.

Nothing is inferred from the drawing. \code{fourfoldplot()} is handed the table,
so the recorded call carries every number the trace wants; the grobs are
consulted only to decide whether the drawing on the page is the one the
recorded call describes.
\subsection{What this deliberately does not do}{

\strong{It is not a \code{mosaic} and it is not a \code{bar}.} A mosaic's tiles tile a
whole and carry proportions of it; these are four independent quarter-discs
separated by \code{space} (default \code{0.2}) that tile nothing. A bar trace would
imply a value axis with a zero baseline and one categorical axis, while
measured the radii are on a square-root scale and the quadrants are laid
out in two dimensions.

\strong{The confidence arcs are dropped.} Measured, a \code{conf.level} draws 8
unfilled arcs (\code{polygon-5} .. \code{polygon-12}), arcs \code{4 + j} and \code{8 + j} being
the lower and upper bound of cell \code{c(tab)[j]}. A \code{heat} layer has nowhere
to carry an interval, so a reader is told the counts and not that the chart
also draws a band around each. Unlike \code{qqplot}'s band this does not justify
declining: the arcs decorate numbers that ARE fully carried.

\strong{The odds ratio is never announced.} There is no MAIDR trace for one
scalar. Under \code{ind.max}/\code{all.max} a reader gets four counts and can compute
it.
}

\subsection{Where the two gates live, and what the second one cannot do}{

#268 asks that a chart whose geometry disagrees LOSE the reading rather
than get a wrong one. That is not achievable here and this does not pretend
it is. Verified against \code{BaseRPlotOrchestrator$initialize()}, which runs
\code{detect_layers()}, \code{resolve_fallback_scope()}, \code{create_layer_processors()}
and \code{process_layers()} at lines 122-125: the picture-versus-chart decision
is frozen by line 123, which reads \code{private$.layers[[i]]$type} through
\code{unsupported_layer_flags()}. That field is written only by the two
\code{private$.layers[[layer_counter]] <- list(type = ...)} assignments at lines
147 and 169, both from \code{detect_layer_type()}, and is never rewritten from a
processor result; line 124 does not even build a processor for a layer
already typed \code{"unknown"} (line 219). A processor that answered
\code{type = "unknown"} would ship that string with \code{has_unsupported_layers()}
still FALSE -- the #214 failure \code{BaseRProcessorFactory$get_supported_types()}
records, where the figure binds and then fails to construct.

So the split is:
\itemize{
\item the \strong{argument} gate is \code{fourfold_decline_reason()}, called from
\code{detect_layer_type()}, and it CAN lose the reading;
\item the \strong{geometry} gate is \code{radii_agree()} below, and it can only return
\code{list()} from \code{generate_selectors()} -- the numbers are still announced
and nothing highlights. That is the shipped pie idiom: "a short list
means these are not this pie's grobs. Filling the gap with a guessed id
would silently highlight another panel's wedges."
}
}

\subsection{What the geometry gate can and cannot see}{

Measured, and weaker than it looks:
\itemize{
\item It is \strong{exactly scale-invariant}. A drawing of \code{tab * 3}, or of
\code{tab * 1e6}, checked against \code{tab}'s counts gives a relative deviation of
\code{1.755e-16} and passes. \code{radius / sqrt(count) == 1 / sqrt(max(tab))} is an
identity inside \code{stdize()}, so no table can falsify it and an upstream
change to what \code{ind.max} means would go uncaught at runtime. That
exposure is carried by the live-drawing assertions in
\code{test-base-r-fourfoldplot.R}, the same bet the \code{qqplot} branch makes.
\item It \strong{passes for a symmetric table drawn under the default \code{std}}.
Measured, \verb{9, 4, 4, 9} under \code{std = "margins"} gives a relative deviation
of \code{0.000e+00}, and so does \verb{7, 7, 7, 7}; algebraically, with
\code{n11 = n22 = a} and \code{n12 = n21 = b}, \verb{u / a == (1 - u) / b == 1 / (a + b)}.
It recovers off symmetry fast -- \verb{9, 4, 4, 9.0001} gives \code{2.778e-06} and
fails at \code{1e-8} -- but this is why \code{std} is the first gate and the
geometry only the second.
}

What it does catch is a grob tree that is not this call's: measured,
\code{fourfoldplot(UCBAdmissions, std = "ind.max")} draws 78 polygons, and an
asymmetric table's \code{margins} drawing checked against its own counts gives
\code{7.616e-01}.
}
