\subsection{\code{BaseRFourfoldLayerProcessor$drawn_dimnames()}}{
  The dimnames \code{fourfoldplot()} itself labels the chart with.

NOT \code{dimnames(recorded_two_way_table(...))}, and the difference is the
whole point. \code{recorded_two_way_table()} repairs dimnames through
\code{as.table()}; \code{graphics::fourfoldplot} repairs them from \code{dimnames(x)}
as handed, after reshaping a 2-D \code{x} to \verb{2 x 2 x 1}. Its own source:

\preformatted{
if (length(dim(x)) == 2L)
    x <- if (is.null(dimnames(x))) array(x, c(dim(x), 1L))
         else array(x, c(dim(x), 1L), c(dimnames(x), list(NULL)))
dnx <- dimnames(x)
if (is.null(dnx)) dnx <- vector("list", 3L)
for (i in which(sapply(dnx, is.null))) dnx[[i]] <- LETTERS[seq_len(dim(x)[i])]
if (is.null(names(dnx))) i <- 1L:3L else i <- which(is.null(names(dnx)))
if (any(i)) names(dnx)[i] <- c("Row", "Col", "Strata")[i]
}

reproduced here rather than approximated. For an \code{ftable} the two
disagree completely: \code{dimnames(ftable(tb))} is NULL, so the chart is
labelled \code{Row: A}, \code{Col: A}, \code{Row: B}, \code{Col: B} -- measured off the
text grobs -- while \code{as.table(ftable(tb))} reconstructs
\code{Treatment}/\code{Outcome} and \code{Drug}/\code{Placebo}/\code{Cured}/\code{Not}. Reading the
levels off \code{as.table()} would announce six strings that appear nowhere
on the page, which is #268's own failure displaced from the numbers
onto the labels.

The \code{which(is.null(names(dnx)))} line is upstream's, quirk included:
\code{is.null()} returns one logical, so that expression is always
\code{integer(0)} and the \code{Row}/\code{Col} default fires only when
\code{names(dimnames(x))} is entirely NULL. A PARTIALLY named \code{dimnames} is
therefore never repaired, and measured, \code{names(dimnames(t)) <- c("Answer", "")} makes the chart literally draw \code{": hi"} and \code{": lo"}.
\code{extract_axis_titles()} substitutes \code{"Col"} there rather than announce
an empty label -- deliberately better than the chart, and the one place
this reading is not identical to it. \code{test-base-r-fourfoldplot.R} pins
the chart's wrong answer as well as ours.
