\subsection{\code{BaseRFourfoldLayerProcessor$extract_axis_titles()}}{
  Name the axes the way the chart places the dimensions.

\code{x} is the SECOND dimension, because measured the columns run left to
right; \code{y} is the first, because the rows run top to bottom. \code{z} names
what the numbers are rather than a dimension of the table -- a reader
told "Outcome" for the value would be given a level name where a number
is.

\code{"Count"} is the contingency-table convention and not a claim that the
values are whole numbers: measured, \code{fourfoldplot()} draws non-integer
cells happily (\verb{1.5, 2.5, 3.5, 4.5} gives radii
\verb{0.5774, 0.7454, 0.8819, 1.0000} and the ratio check passes).

