\subsection{\code{BaseRFourfoldLayerProcessor$wedge_names()}}{
  The four wedge grobs of one fourfold panel, or NULL.

Discriminated by polygon count and vertex count, NOT by \code{gp$fill}.
Fill looks like the exact discriminator and is not: measured,
\code{fourfoldplot(tb, std = "ind.max", color = "steelblue")} leaves two
quadrants with \code{fill = NA}, because \code{fourfoldplot()} does not validate
\code{color}'s length and its draw calls index it as
\code{color[1 + (d > 1)]} / \code{color[2 - (d > 1)]}. A fill-keyed search finds
two wedges there and the layer would ship with no selectors at all, for
a chart that is perfectly readable.

What is measured to be stable:
\itemize{
\item one panel draws \strong{exactly 13} polygons (4 wedges, 8 confidence arcs,
1 frame) or \strong{exactly 5} when \code{conf.level} is \code{0} or \code{FALSE}. The
arc count is 8 or 0 and never anything else -- \code{conf.level} produces
two \verb{for (j in 1:4)} loops guarded by \code{is.numeric(conf.level)}, and
every other value (\code{1}, \code{-0.1}, \code{NA}, a length-2 vector, \code{NULL}) is
rejected by \code{fourfoldplot()} itself. Measured, \code{k} panels draw
\verb{13k} or \verb{5k}: \code{UCBAdmissions} gives 78 and 30, so this count also
refuses another figure's grob tree.
\item the wedges are always the first four in \code{find_graphics_plot_grobs()}
order, because \code{drawPie()} runs before the rings and before the
frame at every \code{conf.level};
\item every quarter disc has \strong{501} vertices (\code{drawPie(n = 500)} plus the
centre point) and the frame is the only \strong{4}-vertex polygon.
}
  \subsection{Usage}{
    \if{html}{\out{<div class="r">}}
    \preformatted{BaseRFourfoldLayerProcessor$wedge_names(gt, plot_index)}
    \if{html}{\out{</div>}}
  }
  \subsection{Arguments}{
    \if{html}{\out{<div class="arguments">}}
    \describe{
      \item{\code{gt}}{Grob tree to search}
      \item{\code{plot_index}}{The plot (panel) index the grob names carry}
    }
    \if{html}{\out{</div>}}
  }
  \subsection{Returns}{
    The four wedge grob names, or NULL when this is not one panel
  }
}

\if{html}{\out{<hr>}}
\if{html}{\out{<a id="method-BaseRFourfoldLayerProcessor-radii_agree"></a>}}
\if{latex}{\out{\hypertarget{method-BaseRFourfoldLayerProcessor-radii_agree}{}}}
\subsection{\code{BaseRFourfoldLayerProcessor$radii_agree()}}{
  Whether the wedges on the page are the counts in the call.

\code{radius / sqrt(count)} constant across the four quadrants, to a
relative spread of \code{1e-8}. Measured headroom on the worst-conditioned
tables that \code{fourfoldplot()} will draw at all --
\code{c(1e-9, 1, 1, 1)} (\code{2.220e-16}), \code{c(1, 2, 3, 1e18)} (\code{2.068e-16}) and
the non-integer \code{c(1.5, 2.5, 3.5, 4.5)} (\code{1.178e-16}) -- is eight
orders below the threshold, while an asymmetric table's \code{margins}
drawing checked against its counts gives \code{7.616e-01}. (\code{c(1, 1e15, 1, 1)} is not in that list because \code{radii_agree()} is never asked about
it -- not because it errors. Measured, \code{fourfoldplot()} returns
normally there with a "NaNs produced" warning and emits ZERO polygon
grobs under \code{ind.max} and \code{all.max}, so \code{wedge_names()} refuses it on
the polygon count first. Under the default \code{margins} the same table
does draw 13 polygons -- radii \verb{0.00017782794, 0.99999998, 0.99999998, 0.00017782794} -- but no \code{margins} call reaches a
processor.)

A zero count makes \code{radius / sqrt(count)} \code{0/0}, so those cells are
held to \code{radius == 0} instead -- measured, a zero cell still emits a
full 501-vertex polygon at the origin, so the grob is there and
addressable.

\strong{The check is vacuous when fewer than two cells are non-zero}: one
non-zero cell makes the relative deviation identically 0 whatever the
radius is. Measured, \verb{0, 0, 0, 160} under \code{ind.max} draws radii
\verb{0, 0, 0, 1} and would pass an unguarded check against any table with
the same three zeros. Two non-zero cells are required before the ratio
is treated as evidence, so such a chart announces its counts and
highlights nothing.
  \subsection{Usage}{
    \if{html}{\out{<div class="r">}}
    \preformatted{BaseRFourfoldLayerProcessor$radii_agree(gt, names, counts)}
    \if{html}{\out{</div>}}
  }
  \subsection{Arguments}{
    \if{html}{\out{<div class="arguments">}}
    \describe{
      \item{\code{gt}}{Grob tree to search}
      \item{\code{names}}{The four wedge grob names, in drawing order}
      \item{\code{counts}}{The four recorded counts, in \code{c(tab)} order}
    }
    \if{html}{\out{</div>}}
  }
  \subsection{Returns}{
    TRUE when the drawing carries the recorded counts
  }
}

\if{html}{\out{<hr>}}
\if{html}{\out{<a id="method-BaseRFourfoldLayerProcessor-generate_selectors"></a>}}
\if{latex}{\out{\hypertarget{method-BaseRFourfoldLayerProcessor-generate_selectors}{}}}
\subsection{\code{BaseRFourfoldLayerProcessor$generate_selectors()}}{
  Address the quadrant the chart drew each count into.

Each quadrant is its own polygon grob, so each needs its own selector
-- the pie's situation, not the barplot's. Two orderings compose here,
and they run in opposite directions:
\itemize{
\item \strong{The drawing is column-major.} Table cell \verb{[r, c]} is polygon
\code{(c - 1) * 2 + r}. Measured on the exported SVG of
\code{fourfoldplot(tb, std = "ind.max")} with \verb{c(tab) = 10, 40, 90, 160},
the polygon centroids in screen coordinates (the root \verb{<g>} carries
\verb{translate(0, 360) scale(1, -1)}, so screen \code{y} is \code{360 - y}) are
\code{polygon-1 (221, 158)} upper LEFT, \code{polygon-2 (190, 224)} lower LEFT,
\code{polygon-3 (345, 114)} upper RIGHT, \code{polygon-4 (376, 268)} lower
RIGHT, and the count labels sit at \code{10 (90, 55)}, \code{40 (90, 305)},
\code{90 (414, 55)}, \code{160 (414, 305)}. So table row 1 is the TOP row on
the page, which is the row \code{extract_data()} emits first.
\item \strong{The \code{heat} trace reverses the numbers and not the selectors.} In
the bundled \verb{maidr-4.8.0/maidr.js} the constructor is
\verb{this.y=[...t.y].reverse(),this.heatmapValues=[...t.points].reverse()},
while \code{mapToSvgElements()}'s array-of-arrays branch walks \code{e[i]}
index for index. Its bare-string branch immediately below DOES
reverse (\verb{let a=t-1-e}), which is what makes the asymmetry easy to
miss. So \code{highlightValues[0]} lines up with \code{points}' LAST row.
}

Grid row 1 is therefore the BOTTOM wedges:
\verb{[[polygon-2, polygon-4], [polygon-1, polygon-3]]}. \strong{The wrong answer
is to emit it index-aligned with \code{points}}, top row first -- it reads
correctly and highlights the vertically mirrored quadrant on every
cell of every fourfold plot: \verb{row 0, col 0} then announces
\code{Placebo / 40} while lighting the wedge drawn for \code{Drug / 10}.
\code{test-base-r-fourfoldplot.R} pins the bottom-first order against that.
\code{image()}/\code{heatmap()} is the precedent -- measured on
\code{image(matrix(1:6, 2))}, \code{points[[1]]} is the top row while
\code{selectors[[1]]} is \code{rect:nth-child(1)}, the lowest rect on the page --
and \code{NEWS.md} records fixing the same mirror there.

Returns \code{list()} when the drawing and the recorded call disagree. The
numbers are still announced -- they are what the call was handed -- and
nothing highlights, rather than a highlight landing on another panel's
quadrant.
  \subsection{Usage}{
    \if{html}{\out{<div class="r">}}
    \preformatted{BaseRFourfoldLayerProcessor$generate_selectors(
  layer_info,
  gt = NULL,
  extracted_data = NULL
)}
    \if{html}{\out{</div>}}
  }
  \subsection{Arguments}{
    \if{html}{\out{<div class="arguments">}}
    \describe{
      \item{\code{layer_info}}{Information about the recorded plot call}
      \item{\code{gt}}{Grob tree to search}
      \item{\code{extracted_data}}{Unused; the counts are re-read from the call}
    }
    \if{html}{\out{</div>}}
  }
  \subsection{Returns}{
    A 2x2 nested list of selectors, or an empty list
  }
}

